<configs>

	<!--
		Learn By Doing levels perks through AddProgressionLevel triggered effects, which
		never touch Progression.SkillPoints. Skill points therefore have nothing left to
		buy once the btnBuy controls are disabled (see XUi_InGame/templates.xml), so stop
		awarding them rather than letting players bank a pile they can never spend.

		This is also a second line of defence: XUiC_SkillPerkLevel.btnBuy_OnPress and
		XUiC_SkillAttributeLevel.btnBuy_OnPress both gate on
		"Progression.SkillPoints >= CalculatedCostForLevel(level)", so with zero points
		a purchase cannot go through even if the button were somehow reachable.

		Caveat: this only stops NEW points from being granted. Points already banked in
		an existing save stay on the character.
	-->
	<set xpath="/progression/level/@skill_points_per_level">0</set>

	<!--
		Third line of defence, and the only one that does not depend on the input path.

		Hiding btnBuy stops the mouse, because an invisible XUiView deactivates its
		GameObject and loses its collider. It does not stop everything: a purchase can
		still be driven through the button's OnPress by other means, and a player with
		points banked from before skill_points_per_level went to zero - or from any other
		source the game hands them - could still level a perk from the keyboard.

		Rather than chase each way in, close the gate they all funnel through.
		XUiC_SkillPerkLevel.btnBuy_OnPress and XUiC_SkillAttributeLevel.btnBuy_OnPress
		both require, among other things:

			CurrentSkill.CostForNextLevel > 0

		CostForNextLevel returns ProgressionClass.CalculatedCostForLevel(Level + 1) for
		anything using SP, and that is

			Mathf.Pow(CostMultiplier, level) * BaseCostToLevel

		so a base cost of zero makes it zero for every level, the condition fails, and the
		handler returns without doing anything - whatever raised it. A cost of zero here
		does not mean "free", it means "not purchasable", which is exactly the intent.

		Scope: these two attributes are the defaults on the containers, so they cover all
		8 attributes and all 59 perks. Crafting skills and books are untouched - they sit
		under different elements, use a different currency, and attBooks and attCrafting
		already ship at zero.

		One cosmetic side effect. XUiC_SkillEntry computes "skillpercentthislevel" as
		1 - CostForNextLevel / CalculatedCostForLevel(level+1), which is now 0/0 for perks
		and attributes. The binding is only rendered when skillpercentshouldshow is true,
		and that is gated on ProgressionType.Skill, so no perk or attribute row displays
		it. It is evaluated but never seen.
	-->
	<set xpath="/progression/attributes/@base_skill_point_cost">0</set>
	<set xpath="/progression/perks/@base_skill_point_cost">0</set>

	<!--
		...and the seven perks that opt out of that calculation entirely.

		CalculatedCostForLevel takes an early exit when a perk declares override_cost:

			if (OverrideCost == null)
				return Mathf.Pow(CostMultiplier, _level) * BaseCostToLevel;   // zeroed above
			if (_level - 1 < OverrideCost.Length)
				return OverrideCost[_level - 1];                              // ignores the above
			return 0;

		So base_skill_point_cost never reaches them and they stayed purchasable. Every
		one is a General perk - Light, Medium and Heavy Armor, Master Chef, Living Off
		The Land, Lock Picking and Lucky Looter, all parented to skillGeneralPerks -
		which is why the hole showed up as "the General tree still lets me spend points".

		A single zero is enough for any level. With override_cost="0" the array has one
		entry, so level 1 reads OverrideCost[0] and every level past it falls through to
		the trailing "return 0". Both give a cost of zero, the > 0 gate fails, and the
		purchase handler does nothing.

		Matched by predicate rather than by name so a perk gaining an override_cost in a
		future patch is covered without anyone noticing it needs to be. As of this build
		that selects exactly the seven above.
	-->
	<set xpath="/progression/perks/perk[@override_cost]/@override_cost">0</set>

	<!--
		Unlock every perk in the tree.

		Learn By Doing does not use attribute prerequisites - a perk levels when you have
		done the thing enough times - but the prerequisites were still there, so perks like
		Fortitude Mastery sat greyed out with "requires Fortitude 6" against them. That is
		misleading on its own, and it is not only cosmetic:

		ProgressionClass.GetCalculatedMaxLevel walks a perk's level_requirements and returns
		the highest level whose requirements currently pass - which is ZERO when the parent
		attribute is too low. SCore's CanPurchasePerk then fails on
		"maxLevel < pv.Level + 1", and 80 files in this modlet gate their level up check on
		exactly that requirement. So a locked perk could not be raised BY LEARN BY DOING
		either, no matter how much XP it had banked. The greyed out entry was telling the
		truth.

		With level_requirements gone, that same method takes its other branch and returns
		the perk's own MaxLevel - 5, from the perks container - so every perk is reachable
		and caps where it always did.

		PERKS ONLY, DELIBERATELY. Attributes carry level_requirements too, but theirs gate
		on PlayerLevel rather than on another attribute, which is a constraint worth
		keeping. The branch above also returns a flat 20 for anything IsAttribute when the
		list is empty, so stripping them would quietly raise the attribute cap from 10 to
		20.
	-->
	<remove xpath="/progression/perks/perk/level_requirements"/>

</configs>